<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Emirate of Hakkari</h1><p class="st-meta">1384 CE – 1845 CE (approx.) · capital Julamerk</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Kurdish emirate in the mountains south-east of Lake Van, ruling Kurds and Assyrian Christians for nearly five centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Hakkari mountains had long been held by Kurdish lords. In the late 14th century a dynasty based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julamerk">Julamerk</a> (Colemerik) emerged; its emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-din-shir">Izz al-Din Shir</a> submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> and kept his lands, and his family ruled the region thereafter.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> ruled a remote mountain land of Kurdish tribes and semi-autonomous Assyrian Christian tribes whose patriarch lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qudshanis">Qudshanis</a>. After 1514 they became Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> with hereditary rule, sometimes switching to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>. Revenues came from flocks, tribute and the roads between Van and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 19th century Ottoman centralisation and the revolt of the neighbouring emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedir-khan-beg">Bedir Khan of Bohtan</a>, in which Hakkari's emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurullah-bey">Nurullah Bey</a> took part, led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>'s abolition in the mid 1840s.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1384 CE – 1845 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.24%;width:9.17%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1384 CE – 1845 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Julamerk</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Izz al-Din Shir, Nurullah Bey</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kurdish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hakkari emirate, Hakkari Kurdish emirate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1614&amp;focus=emirate-of-hakkari" aria-label="Emirate of Hakkari on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5543 761 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Emirate of Hakkari lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5863 1017h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5863 1017h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Turkey. <a href="/atlas?year=1614&amp;focus=emirate-of-hakkari">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

